{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Learning Experience Design and Software Technology","title":"AI Tutor Boundaries","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting ai tutor boundaries to AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_learning_experience_design_and_software_technology","ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"05","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/02/04"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_calibration"]},"content":"# AI Tutor Boundaries\n\n![AI Tutor Boundaries textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/02-05-ai-tutor-boundaries.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen an AI tutor helps you design, it can occupy one of several roles: a generator that produces the artifact, an advisor that proposes options, a critic that questions your reasoning, or an oracle whose claims you accept as true. The boundary you draw around that role determines whether the AI accelerates your judgment or replaces it. This section is about drawing those boundaries on purpose and recording them, so that \"I used AI\" becomes an account a reviewer can audit rather than a vague admission.\n\nThe governing distinction is between the AI making a *proposal* and the AI making a *decision*. A proposal enters your reasoning as one option among others, still subject to your evidence and your goal; a decision is what you alone are accountable for. Boundaries also cover *verification* (who checks the AI's factual claims against course evidence) and *disclosure* (a record specific enough that another person could re-trace what the AI touched and what you did with it). A blanket \"I used AI to improve my storyboard\" violates all three: it hides which decision the AI touched, whether anyone verified its claims, and where the human judgment was.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAn AI tutor reviews a designer's onboarding flow and suggests merging Frames 2 and 3 to \"reduce clicks.\" It sounds authoritative and the rationale is plausible. The designer almost accepts it, then checks the pilot drop-off data and finds that Frame 3 is exactly where five of twelve learners first interact with the interface. Merging the frames would bury that action cue. The tutor was confidently wrong, not maliciously but because it optimized a proxy (clicks) it could see while blind to the learning evidence it could not.\n\nThe case shows why the boundary matters more than the suggestion. The danger was never that the AI made a proposal; proposals are useful. The danger was the gravitational pull to treat the proposal as a verdict because it arrived fluent and certain. The designer held the verification boundary, checked the claim against course evidence, and recorded the rejection. The disclosure of that exchange, not the polish of the final flow, is what makes the work defensible.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn AI tutor boundary has three components, and a defensible disclosure makes all three visible. The **role boundary** fixes whether the AI proposes or decides; in learning design the AI should stay a proposer and a critic, never the final authority on what serves the learner. The **verification boundary** fixes who is accountable for the truth of the AI's claims; because generative tools produce fluent text regardless of accuracy, every factual or empirical claim (\"this cuts three clicks,\" \"learners prefer X\") must be checked against course evidence before it can justify a decision. The **disclosure boundary** fixes the granularity of the record; an auditable disclosure names the one decision the AI touched, what it proposed, what evidence you checked it against, and what you accepted or rejected and why.\n\nWhy insist on a *bounded* disclosure tied to a single decision rather than a blanket statement? Because accountability is only legible at the level of a specific choice. \"I used AI to improve my storyboard\" cannot be evaluated; \"the tutor proposed merging Frames 2 and 3, I checked it against drop-off data showing Frame 3 is the first interaction point, and I rejected it\" can be evaluated, challenged, and learned from.\n\nThe course anchors do real work in setting these boundaries:\n\n- **Cognitive load theory** is the standard a proposal must meet: a suggestion to \"reduce clicks\" is only good if the clicks were extraneous load, not the germane interaction the frame exists to create.\n- **UDL learner variability** flags when an AI's efficiency optimization quietly removes an accessibility provision, since usage-based proposals systematically undervalue features used by a minority of learners.\n- **Mayer's principles** give you the evidence base to push back on a confident but wrong suggestion, replacing \"the AI said so\" with \"the redundancy principle says otherwise.\"\n\nA defensible disclosure makes three boundaries visible. Match each boundary to what it actually fixes.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Role boundary\",\"definition\":\"Fixes whether the AI proposes or decides; in learning design the AI stays a proposer and critic, never the final authority\"},{\"term\":\"Verification boundary\",\"definition\":\"Fixes who is accountable for the truth of the AI claim; every factual claim is checked against course evidence before it justifies a decision\"},{\"term\":\"Disclosure boundary\",\"definition\":\"Fixes the granularity of the record; it names the one decision the AI touched, the proposal, the evidence checked, and the accept-or-reject reason\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A learner writes only: 'I used AI to help improve my storyboard.' Which boundary does this disclosure fail to make visible?\" explanation=\"The blanket statement is not false, but it names no single decision, no proposal, no evidence check, and no verdict, so a reviewer cannot re-trace what the AI touched or where the human judgment was. It fails the disclosure boundary (granularity), and by omission obscures the role and verification boundaries too.\" options='[{\"text\":\"None; the statement is honest, so the boundary is met\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The disclosure boundary, because it names no specific decision, proposal, evidence, or verdict a reviewer could check\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Only the role boundary, because it does not say the AI decided\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: An Annotated AI-Use Disclosure\n\nHere is a before-and-after of one AI-use disclosure excerpt tied to a single bounded design-support decision.\n\n**Before (blanket disclosure)**\n\n> I used AI to help improve my storyboard.\n\nThis is not auditable. It does not say which decision the AI touched, what it proposed, what evidence the learner checked it against, or what was accepted or rejected.\n\n**The decision point.** The tutor had suggested merging onboarding Frames 2 and 3 to \"reduce clicks.\" Pilot drop-off data showed that Frame 3 was where 5 of 12 learners first acted on the interface. Merging would have buried that action cue.\n\n**After (bounded, auditable disclosure)**\n\n> AI-use note: I asked the tutor for one way to shorten my onboarding flow. It proposed merging Frames 2 and 3 to cut a click. I checked this against my pilot drop-off data, which showed Frame 3 is where 5 of 12 learners first interacted. I rejected the merge because it would hide that action cue, and I verified the tutor's claim about click count against the actual frame map rather than taking it as authoritative.\n\nNotice the boundaries this disclosure makes visible: the tutor offered a proposal, not a verdict (it did not get to decide); the learner held the verification boundary (checking the claim against course evidence); and the disclosure names the one bounded decision, the evidence, and the reject reason. That is what turns \"I used AI\" into an account a reviewer can audit.\n\nWork the upgrade from blanket to bounded disclosure one boundary at a time, predicting each before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Upgrading the AI-use disclosure\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The tutor says merging Frames 2 and 3 will cut a click and sounds authoritative. Before acting, what does the verification boundary require?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Accept it, since reducing clicks is generally good\",\"Check the click-reduction claim against the actual frame map and the learning evidence\",\"Ask the tutor to confirm it is sure\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Fluency carries no information about accuracy. The verification boundary requires checking the claim against course evidence: here, the drop-off data showing Frame 3 is where 5 of 12 learners first act.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The data shows merging would bury the first interaction cue. What does recording the rejection demonstrate?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"That the learner was uncooperative with the tool\",\"That the human held the role boundary, keeping the AI a proposer rather than a decider\",\"That AI suggestions should never be used\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A logged rejection proves the AI proposed and the human decided. A record of only accepted AI input cannot show the boundary was held; the rejection is the proof of judgment.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The bounded disclosure is longer than the blanket one not because it confesses more, but because it makes the human judgment legible: one decision, one proposal, one evidence check, one verdict, each re-traceable by a reviewer.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTreat every AI suggestion as a proposal to be tested against your learning goal and your evidence, never as a verdict to be applied. The tool may speak first, but the human verifies and decides last.\n\nBefore you accept an AI tutor's suggestion or finalize your disclosure, run these checks:\n\n1. **Role:** Did the AI propose, or did you let it decide? If you cannot name the alternative you chose between, the AI decided for you.\n2. **Verification:** Did you check the AI's factual or empirical claim against course evidence, or accept its fluency as authority?\n3. **Goal fit:** Does the suggestion serve the learning goal, or only a proxy the tool can measure (clicks, time, completion)?\n4. **Disclosure granularity:** Does your record name the one decision, the proposal, the evidence, and the accept/reject reason, so a reviewer could re-trace it?\n\nA disclosure that passes is auditable. \"I used AI to help\" fails every check and tells a reviewer nothing about where your judgment was.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading treats disclosure as a confession of guilt, so the goal becomes minimizing what you admit (\"I only used it a little\"). Disclosure is not an apology; it is documentation of where a tool entered your reasoning and how your judgment governed it. A precise disclosure of substantial, well-verified AI use is more professional than a vague denial.\n\nThe second misreading equates the AI's fluency with authority, so a confident suggestion feels like a finding. Generative tools produce equally fluent text whether they are right or wrong; fluency carries no information about accuracy. The boundary that protects you is verification against course evidence, which is exactly what the worked example's designer did when checking the click-count claim against the actual frame map.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nRead the two disclosures in the worked example side by side as a study in granularity. The blanket version (\"I used AI to help improve my storyboard\") is not false; it is simply unauditable, because nothing in it can be checked or challenged. The bounded version names the single decision (shortening the onboarding flow), the proposal (merge Frames 2 and 3), the evidence the designer checked it against (drop-off data showing Frame 3 is the first interaction point for five of twelve learners), and the reasoned rejection. The second is longer not because it confesses more but because it makes the human judgment legible.\n\nTrace where each boundary appears in that disclosure. The role boundary is visible in the phrase \"it proposed,\" which keeps the AI a proposer rather than a decider. The verification boundary is visible in \"I verified the tutor's claim about click count against the actual frame map rather than taking it as authoritative.\" The disclosure boundary is the structure itself: one decision, one proposal, one evidence check, one verdict. Together they convert a vague admission into an account a reviewer can audit.\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that good AI use and good disclosure are the same skill seen from two sides. You cannot write a bounded disclosure unless you actually maintained the boundaries while working, and maintaining the boundaries while working is what produces a defensible design.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a running log of every AI suggestion you receive in your own work and tag each as proposed-and-verified, proposed-and-rejected, or accepted-without-checking. The third category is the one to watch: it is where the AI quietly crossed from advisor to decider. Most boundary failures are not dramatic; they are small, unverified acceptances that accumulate until the design is no longer yours.\n\nA strong reader of AI tutor boundaries can answer four questions about any AI-assisted decision:\n\n1. Did the AI propose or decide, and can I name the alternative I chose between?\n2. What course evidence did I check the AI's claim against before acting on it?\n3. Did the suggestion serve the learning goal or only a proxy the tool could measure?\n4. Could a reviewer re-trace this exact exchange from my disclosure alone?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong artifact for this section is a disclosure tied to exactly one bounded decision, naming the proposal, the evidence check, and the accept-or-reject reason. The strongest disclosures include a rejected suggestion, because a record that only shows accepted AI input cannot demonstrate that the human held the boundary; the rejection is the proof of judgment.\n\nWhen you use BigAL here, use it deliberately as the proposer so you can practice the boundary in real time, then disclose the exchange. A useful move is: \"Propose one change to this onboarding flow and state the assumption your proposal depends on.\" Asking for the underlying assumption gives you the exact thing to verify against your evidence, and it makes the role boundary explicit from the first turn.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same boundary practice from a graduate design course, where disclosure is mostly about academic integrity, to a workplace where you ship learning products under a client contract and a privacy regulation. The role and verification boundaries hold, but the stakes and the verification sources change. In the workplace, an AI suggestion to \"personalize using learner data\" is not just possibly wrong about learning; it may violate the contract or the privacy rule, so the verification boundary now includes legal and policy evidence, not only pedagogical evidence. A disclosure that satisfied a professor (\"I checked it against drop-off data\") may be insufficient for a client who needs to know whether learner data left the system. If you can explain why the same three boundaries require a wider evidence base in the regulated workplace, you understand AI tutor boundaries as a transferable accountability discipline rather than a course formality.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain in your own words why an AI suggestion's fluency carries no information about its accuracy, and what the learner must therefore do before acting on it.\" answer=\"Generative tools produce equally fluent, confident text whether the underlying claim is true or false, because fluency is a property of how the text is generated, not of whether it matches reality. A polished suggestion to 'merge frames to cut a click' reads identically whether or not it harms learning. So fluency cannot be used as evidence. The learner must hold the verification boundary: check the factual or empirical claim against course evidence (here, the drop-off data) before letting it justify a decision, and record that check in the disclosure.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/02-05-ai-tutor-boundaries-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"02.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning (3rd ed.)](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316941355)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision for AIL 606 02.05. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links AI Tutor Boundaries, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer, R. E. (2020). [Multimedia Learning (3rd ed.)](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316941355). Cambridge University Press.\n- Mayer, R. E., and Fiorella, L. (Eds.) (2021). The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning (3rd ed.). Cambridge University Press. https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- Sweller, van Merriënboer, and Paas (2019). Cognitive architecture and instructional design: 20 years later. Educational Psychology Review, 31(2), 261-292. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-019-09465-5\n- CAST (2024). Universal Design for Learning Guidelines version 3.0. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries","stem":"A learner asks the embedded AI tutor, \"What is the answer to problem 4?\" Which tutor behavior best respects a learning boundary?","options":["Give the final answer so the learner can move on quickly.","Withhold the final answer and instead ask which step the learner is stuck on, then offer the next move.","Refuse to respond to any question about problem 4.","Give the answer but add a disclaimer that the learner should not rely on it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A learning tutor's boundary keeps the learner doing the thinking. It withholds the final answer and offers a scaffolded next step instead.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_tutor_should_answer_everything","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries","stem":"Which AI-use disclosure is auditable rather than a blanket statement?","options":["AI tools were used during this assignment.","I asked the tutor to suggest three alternative phrasings for my rubric's top level; I adopted one, reworded it to match my course language, and rejected two for being vague.","This work is partly AI-assisted.","AI helped me think about the project."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An auditable disclosure names the bounded decision the AI touched, what it proposed, and what was accepted or rejected. A blanket sentence cannot be inspected.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_disclosure_is_blanket","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries","stem":"An AI tutor gives a confident, well-written explanation of a learning theory that a learner has never seen. What boundary should the design enforce before the learner relies on it?","options":["None; if the explanation is fluent and confident, it is trustworthy.","A verification step that checks the tutor's claim against the assigned course material, because fluency is not the same as accuracy.","Block the tutor from explaining any theory at all.","Require the learner to rate the explanation's writing quality."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fluent, confident output can still be wrong. A verification boundary against course evidence protects the learner from authoritative-sounding error.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_fluent_means_correct","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_tutor_boundaries"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your draft disclosure reads: \"I used AI to improve my storyboard.\" An instructor flags it as not auditable. Which revision best fixes it?","options":["Add the brand name of the AI tool you used.","Rewrite it to name the one bounded decision (the tutor suggested merging two onboarding frames), the evidence you checked it against (the pilot drop-off data), and your verdict (rejected, because merging hid the action cue).","Delete the disclosure so it no longer draws attention.","Expand it to a full paragraph describing how impressive the AI is."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A defensible disclosure ties a specific bounded decision to the evidence used to judge the AI's suggestion and records the accept-or-reject verdict.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_disclosure_is_blanket","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_tutor_should_answer_everything","pattern":"tutor_as_answer_engine","description":"Designing the AI tutor to give the final answer on demand instead of holding a boundary that keeps the learner doing the thinking","trigger":"learner specifies a tutor that solves the problem when asked rather than scaffolding the next step","feedback":"A learning tutor's boundary is to support the learner's thinking, not replace it. Specify what the tutor will withhold (the final answer) and what it will offer (a next step or question).","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_disclosure_is_blanket","pattern":"disclosure_as_blanket_statement","description":"Treating an AI-use disclosure as a generic blanket sentence rather than a specific account of which decision the AI touched","trigger":"learner writes AI was used in this work without naming the bounded decision, the prompt, and what was accepted or rejected","feedback":"A useful disclosure names the specific bounded decision the AI informed, what it proposed, and what you accepted or rejected. A blanket sentence is not auditable.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_2_5_ai_tutor_boundaries_fluent_means_correct","pattern":"fluent_output_assumed_accurate","description":"Assuming a confident, fluent tutor response is accurate and pedagogically appropriate without checking it against course evidence","trigger":"learner accepts a tutor explanation because it sounds authoritative, with no verification step in the design","feedback":"Fluency is not accuracy. Build a verification boundary so the tutor's output is checked against course material before a learner relies on it.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"167010695b5367e13b7f57a5b6e49f9127471d4902bfe31de71c42c40615afc5","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}